CHANGE 5 — LOCAL SIMULATION VIEWER (no cloud, no credentials)
  GAZEBO/portal/viewer/index.html - a local HTML page that connects to
  ws://127.0.0.1:8081 and renders the camera on a canvas. Served by the portal
  at http://127.0.0.1:8765/viewer.
  WHY: https://app.foxglove.dev is a HOSTED CLOUD APPLICATION behind an account
  wall (verified: it redirects to /signin and shows "Need an account?"). Using
  it would send the simulation's camera feed to a third party. The local viewer
  keeps everything on the host.
  CREDENTIALS: none. A loopback WebSocket to a bridge on 127.0.0.1 has no
  account, token or password, so there is nothing to store. Nothing was written
  to KDE Wallet, deliberately - storing a credential with no consumer would be
  worse than storing none.

  THREE REAL BUGS FOUND AND FIXED WHILE BUILDING IT
  1. THE BRIDGE NEVER RECEIVED ANYTHING. Two causes, both fixed:
     a. The Gazebo server advertised GZ_IP=127.0.0.1, so its camera publisher
        was reachable only on loopback. The GUI was unaffected only because it
        shares the server's network namespace. Measured from the bridge:
          gz topic -i -t /factory/camera/image
            No publishers on topic [/factory/camera/image]
        i.e. the subscriber connected and no data ever arrived.
        FIX: server now advertises a routable, PINNED address, 10.89.5.10, with
        PodmanArgs=--ip 10.89.5.10. Must be changed in both places together.
        After: Publishers [tcp://10.89.5.10:36593, gz.msgs.Image]
     b. The bridge entrypoint set GZ_PARTITION but NOT GZ_IP. Unset, gz-transport
        falls back to the container hostname, which resolves to nothing
        reachable. FIX: entrypoint now derives GZ_IP at runtime from the
        container's own ao-sim-fabrication address and fails fast if absent:
          [entrypoint] gz-transport discovery will advertise GZ_IP=10.89.5.37
  2. THE VIEWER SHOWED NOTHING THOUGH DATA WAS ARRIVING. The bridge sends CDR
     message data as BINARY WebSocket frames, not JSON. Measured: 97 binary
     frames in 10s, 2 text frames. JSON.parse threw on every one and the
     failure was invisible - it looks exactly like "connected but blank".
  3. THE CDR DECODER WAS WRONG TWICE. The bridge prepends a variable-length
     preamble (measured 17 bytes, carrying the subscription id) ahead of the
     CDR payload, so a fixed offset is wrong. The decoder is now SELF-LOCATING:
     it finds the "rgb8" encoding field and reads every other field relative to
     it, with a length check before drawing. Verified against a live frame:
       height 240 @65, width 320 @69, "rgb8" @77, step 960 @85, data @93
       230493 - 93 == 240*320*3 == 230400
     A scope bug (returning `c` instead of `p.s` inside a map callback) threw a
     ReferenceError that the per-frame catch swallowed. Caught by calling
     decodeImage() directly on a live frame rather than trusting the page.

  VERIFIED IN A BROWSER (not by assertion):
    http://127.0.0.1:8765/viewer
      res = "320 x 240", enc = "rgb8", hz = "10.0 Hz", age = "just now"
    Pixel samples across the frame return real rendered values (188-218 grey,
    sky/ground gradient), so it is image content and not a blank canvas.
    The scene is STATIC between frames because factory.world has no animated
    entities; frames still arrive at the world's update_rate of 10 Hz.

