CHANGE 11 - README work item 83 closed: rl_objects now exists in the world
Defect: objects.yaml declared 9 trainable objects in 3 groups and named a
non-static model `rl_objects` as their home, but no such model was in
factory.world. /api/objects therefore served a catalogue of objects Gazebo
had never instantiated, while Section 10.2.1 requires them to be
individually addressable, observable and resettable.

Fix: scripts/simulation/build-rl-objects.py renders objects.yaml into the
world. Generating one from the other is deliberate - the defect WAS a silent
disagreement between two files, and hand editing cannot stop that recurring.
The block is fenced by BEGIN/END markers, is idempotent (--write twice is a
no-op), and --check fails if the world is stale, so a future catalogue edit
cannot silently diverge again.

Traps hit while building it, all recorded in the script:
1. Appending the block to the end of the file lands it AFTER </sdf>, which is
   "junk after the document element" and fails to parse. It must be spliced
   in before </world>.
2. Each link MUST carry its home_pose. The model sits at the world origin, so
   links left at 0 0 0 stack every object on the origin. This produced no log
   error at all - the world loaded clean and the objects were simply not where
   the catalogue said. Only a screenshot revealed it.
3. objects.yaml writes home_pose as [x, y, z, "0 0 0"] - the trailing
   roll/pitch/yaw is one flow STRING inside the list, so a naive float() over
   the list raises ValueError.

Each object gets collision plus explicit solid-box inertia, so the engine
never has to infer inertia from collision geometry (the usual source of the
"missing <inertial>" warning on small dynamic bodies in gz-sim 10).

Portal /api/objects now reports in_world {known, present, missing} so a
consumer can tell catalogue from simulation instead of trusting the catalogue.

Verified: 9 objects / 3 groups in the world, 8 cameras still advertised, world
loads with no errors, objects visible and settled (frame identical after 15 s),
in_world present=9 missing=[], portal still view-only (POST 405, health
view_only true), simulation start time unchanged by any of this.

CHANGE 12 - IMAGE QUALITY: sensors 320x240 -> 640x480; zone visuals off by default
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Operator reported image quality in the viewer as very bad. Measured and fixed:

1. Root cause of the blur was NOT sensor resolution but double resampling. The
   viewer defaulted to a 640x480 frame while sensors emitted 320x240, so every
   frame was upscaled with smooth interpolation. Sensors are now 640x480 and the
   viewer defaults to 640x480, giving a 1:1 pixel mapping and no resampling.

2. 1280x960 was tried and REVERTED. It dropped the frame rate to 0.9 Hz and drove
   gz-sim-server to 700% CPU. Measured ladder:
       320x240 -> 2.00 Hz, 313% CPU
       640x480 -> 1.60 Hz, 493% CPU
      1280x960 -> 0.90 Hz, 700% CPU
   Rendering is headless software rasterisation (llvmpipe; Ogre2RenderEngine.cc:556
   "Unable to open display ... headless mode"), so it is CPU-bound and starves
   under contention. Host load was 11.58 on 12 cores with four concurrent cline
   sessions, which is why the original 320x240 baseline also measures ~2 Hz now
   rather than the 10 Hz recorded earlier in the day. Scene geometry is unchanged
   (12 models, 15 meshes), so this is host contention, not a regression in the world.

3. Red wash over every camera was self-inflicted. The safety-zone work (CHANGE 10)
   emitted translucent zone volumes; zone-fabrication-floor is a
   9.874 x 6.8311 x 4.0574 m box enclosing the whole building, so it tinted every
   frame flat red. Lighting was verified untouched (git diff empty for ambient/
   diffuse/direction). Zone rendering is now opt-in behind the --visual flag in
   verify_safety_zones.py; the zone links and poses remain in the SDF so the zone
   model stays machine-readable and verifiable.

TRAP: an XML comment may not contain "--". Emitting "pass --visual to draw zones"
produced "not well-formed (invalid token): line 973" and Gazebo would not have
loaded the world. The flag name had to be reworded out of the comment text.

Verified: world XML valid, 8 cameras advertised, zone tint materials 0, zone
links 4, portal/bridge/gz all active. Viewer shows the approved palette (tan
walls, blue conveyor, orange arms) with the rl_objects bins visible.

CHANGE 13 - RENDER QUALITY: lighting rebalance, material shininess, bogus gear
visuals removed. 2026-10-03
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Operator: "MAKE THE RENDERING LOOK GOOD, IT LOOKS AWFUL".

1. Lighting. Scene ambient was 0.62. In Gazebo <ambient> is ADDED FLAT, so a
   value that high lifted every shadowed face almost to the lit faces and
   flattened the whole building into one tone. Dropped to 0.20, which was too
   dark, and settled at 0.34 with the fill light lifted to 0.52 0.58 0.70.
   Key light warmed to 1.0 0.95 0.86. Background changed from flat 0.7 grey to
   0.55 0.62 0.72 so the backdrop is not dead grey.

2. Materials. NO material in the world had <shininess>, so every specular
   highlight was the same broad plastic blob. Added shininess tuned per surface:
   walls 6 (matte painted block), conveyor belt 32, arms 38, ground 4,
   RL bins 24, camera markers 45. Per-material ambient was also far too high
   (walls 0.30 against diffuse 0.44) and was cancelling the directional shading;
   ambient is now low on every material and the lights do the modelling.

3. Removed the 12 conveyor_gear_NN visuals. TRAP AND SELF-INFLICTED DEFECT: an
   earlier change claimed to have extracted conveyor gearboxes as silver parts.
   split-collada-parts.py had actually produced twelve copies of ONE part - all
   twelve share the same 4x5x3 inch signature and the same local centre
   (2.00, 2.50, 3.50) in, with no node transform. At scale 0.0254 they all land
   within 10 cm of the world origin, 6.02 m from the conveyor at
   (6.073, 2.337, 1.029) that they were meant to dress. They drew a stray
   light-coloured sliver through the wall and cost twelve extra draws per frame
   for geometry representing nothing. Removed. The blue conveyor still renders
   from conveyor.dae.

TRAP - ElementTree DESTROYS XML COMMENTS. Rewriting factory.world with
xml.etree.ElementTree stripped all 61 comments from the file. It was caught on
the next check (grep -c '<!--' returned 0) and restored from backup. The
material edit was then redone with string/regex replacement only, asserting the
comment count before and after every edit. NEVER round-trip a hand-commented SDF
file through ElementTree.

Verified: world XML valid, 8 cameras advertised, 0 mesh load failures, no
conveyor_gear references remain, comments 62 -> 50 (the 12 removed gears each
carried one explanatory comment), shininess blocks 27 -> 15. All three units
active. Screenshot confirms: no stray sliver, tan walls with tonal separation,
