Backups: /tmp/fw.pre-flat, /tmp/viewer.pre-edge.

CHANGE 17 - 2026-10-04 - Massing shaded emissive-only (flat panels)

SYMPTOM: operator reported each flat plane reads as multiple polygons and is
shaded badly because of it.

MEASURED - the export really is subdivided. massing_fab.dae:
  triangles            28288 in 196 triangle sets across 98 geometries
  unique vertex triples 28288  ->  reuse 1.00x (already fully split, so this is
                                  NOT shared-vertex normal interpolation)
  explicit <normal>     0 / 28288  ->  the engine derives face normals
  normal spread within an axis-aligned bucket: up to 2.80 degrees
  planar grouping (0.5 deg / 2 mm): 5866 groups, avg 4.8 triangles per panel
So panels are subdivided roughly 2-5x beyond a single quad, and those triangles
differ by a couple of degrees of normal, which is what bands them under a
directional light.

FIX APPLIED: the massing material now carries its tone in <emissive> with
ambient/diffuse/specular all zero, so no light direction and no per-triangle
normal can modulate it. The tone is exactly flat regardless of how the panel was
triangulated, and form comes entirely from the viewer's edge overlay. Arms,
conveyors and gear keep their normal lit materials.

ALSO REJECTED: a mesh-rewrite route (flatten-panel-normals.py, park all
coplanar triangles onto one averaged normal) was written and measured correctly
but the emitted DAE came out malformed - the Collada file's namespace handling
did not survive ElementTree round-tripping, and the <vertices> element picked up
a stray NORMAL input. Derived mesh deleted, script parked at
/tmp/flatten-panel-normals.py.broken. Not shipped rather than shipped broken.
If true single-polygon panels are still wanted, the right route is to weld the
coplanar groups in the source CAD and re-export, not to post-process the DAE.

VIEWER: Sobel ink capped 0.97 -> 0.80 and range 5..46 -> 9..52. At 0.97 nearly
every detected pixel went black, which turned the small arms and conveyor
details into solid blobs; they are mostly edge at that pixel scale. Trade-off
remains: fine detail is inked. The "edge lines" checkbox gives the un-inked
lit view.

CHANGE 18 - 2026-10-04 - Flat-shaded structure mesh; panels read as single planes

SYMPTOM (round 1): panels read as multiple polygons, shaded badly.
SYMPTOM (round 2, after emissive-only): all white, and now many panel edges
invisible - the Sobel had no luminance step to find between adjacent panels.
Round 2 was an over-correction: removing all shading variation removed the very
signal the edge overlay depends on.

TRUE CAUSE, measured. The mesh carries SMOOTHED VERTEX NORMALS, not face
normals. <vertices> binds a NORMAL source alongside POSITION, and vertex INDICES
ARE SHARED between triangles (an earlier 1.00x "reuse" reading was wrong - it
counted unique index TRIPLES, not individual indices). Corner normals within one
triangle disagreed by up to 56 degrees, so the engine interpolated a gradient
across each triangle.

TWO FAILED ROUTES, recorded so they are not retried:
  1. Averaging normals per planar group and writing them into the existing NORMAL
     arrays in place. Result: triangles with corner disagreement only 11436 ->
     10915, and worst disagreement WORSENED to 180 deg. Cause: indices are shared,
     so two coplanar and two non-coplanar triangles fight over the same vertex
     slot and the last writer wins with the opposite normal.
  2. ElementTree round-trip of the whole Collada document to add a new NORMAL
     source. Result: malformed output - namespace handling did not survive and
     <vertices> picked up a stray NORMAL input. Deleted rather than shipped.

FIX THAT WORKED: drop the NORMAL binding from <vertices> entirely
(flatten-panel-normals.py). With no vertex normals the engine derives a face
normal per triangle, so every triangle is flat-shaded. Coplanar triangles in one
panel differ by well under a degree, so the panel reads as ONE surface, while
genuinely different panels keep clearly different normals - which is exactly the
signal the edge overlay needs. The transform rewrites no elements, so there is no
namespace risk.

  meshes/massing_flat.dae  derived; source export massing_fab.dae untouched.
  Parses clean; 28288 triangles preserved; 101 vertex NORMAL inputs removed.
  factory.world points the structure visual at the derived mesh.
  Material restored from emissive-only to lit: ambient 0.34, diffuse 0.58,
  specular 0.03. At 0.58/0.74 every panel blew to near-white and the
  per-orientation separation collapsed again.

Verified in the viewer: no within-panel gradients, panel-to-panel boundaries all
visible, arms and conveyor legible.
